{"id":"pyroclastic_explosion","compound_name":"Graphs::Aero::pyroclastic_explosion","source_path":"pyroclastic_explosion/pyroclastic_explosion.json","category":"Fire","author":"Autodesk","description":"This graph shows how to use Bifrost Aero to create a large, pyroclastic explosion.","documentation_file":"pyroclastic_explosion_docs_ENU.md","documentation":"## Aero Pyroclastic Explosion\n\n### Description\n\nThis graph shows how to use Bifrost Aero to create a large, pyroclastic explosion.  The details seen in the large plume of smoke are also representative  of a volcano's pyroclastic flow. In this case, the effect is created with a combustion reaction in Aero and additonal influences.\n\nThis graph takes a geometry, displaces it with animated noise and uses that to emit both smoke and fuel in two separate  sources. The dual sources allow finer control over the combustion, movement and density of the smoke.\n\nThe signature highly detailed smoke in this large explosion is primarily created with the `aero_pyroclastic_influence`. Further control over the look and influence strength is demonstrated by an input to the vorticity port. Over time, an fcurve is used to determine how strong to apply the vorticity. Generally, higher vorticity at the start of the explosion then less over time yields the best result. Additional influences control a simulated ground collision as well as how quickly the smoke dissipates over time.\n\nAfter the simulation there is a `file_cache` node to provide the ideal location to cache out the simulation. As an optional step, one can enable `post_refine_aero` to run a post process to upres the simulation further. Note that the post refine will require more system memory since the resolution will be at least doubled from the simulation.\n\n### Inputs, Controls & Outputs\n\nThere are several levels of control for this graph.\n\nThe source geometry displacement and source direction are controlled with a fractal turbulence field in the \"noise\" compound at the beginning.\n\nThe fog density and temperature are controlled by vary_source_property compounds.\n\nThe resolution of the aero simulation is controlled by a value node that has been annotated with \"Fluid detail size\". The advantage of the value node is using it for the fluid_detail_size port on both sources feeding into the Aero node. While it is possible to prototype the simulation at a lower resolution for quicker iterations, combustion reactions will vary. To achieve the same performance at a higher resolution is where the `post_refine_aero` workflow shines.\n\nFinally, there is an `Arnold_standard_volume_mat` node to define the material for both display and render.  The Arnold render had the best results when the interpolation was set to tricubic.\n\n### Best Practices and Tips\n\n* For quicker simulation times and iterations one can use a lower resolution (larger value for the fluid detail size). However, combustion reactions can differ a large amount across resolutions. Once the effect is roughly dialed in, it is ideal to keep the detail size the same to achieve a consistent performance. Use the post refine workflow for added resolution with a known performance.\n\n* Large scale simulations can be costly to simulate. Directly after the simulation is an ideal place to save out your expensive compute. Once the simulation is safely cached to disk, it is much easier to modify the shader or experiment with post processes without risk of losing that simulation data. Included in the graph is a recommended point to cache the simulation. The `file_cache` is set to \"passthrough\" by default so the result flows through the graph to the output once it is loaded. Change this to write, read or lazy modes as it suits you.\n\n* Work efficiently. Volumetric simulation data can consume large amounts of disk space. Ensure that only required voxel channels are saved out to disk. Not only will the cached files be smaller, but they will also read and write quicker. The required properties to render the explosion are: voxel_fog_density, voxel_temperature, and voxel_velocity.","library_dependencies":{"Amino":"2.15.0.9","bif":"2.15.0.9","fields":"2.15.0.9","file":"2.15.0.9","fluids":"2.15.0.9","geometries":"2.15.0.9","scatter":"2.15.0.9","simulation":"2.15.0.9"},"graph_ports":[{"portName":"aero_volume","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"}],"terminal_states":[{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::final","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::proxy","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::diagnostic","enabled":"false"}],"counts":{"nodes":48,"connections":64,"values":141,"compounds":4},"node_types":{"Core::Graph::pass":6,"Value::float":3,"Core::Time::time":3,"Simulation::Aero::source_air":2,"Rendering::Materials::assign_material":2,"Simulation::Common::vary_source_property":2,"Core::FCurve::evaluate_fcurve":2,"Core::Math::multiply":2,"Core::Math::divide":2,"Simulation::Aero::aero_solver_settings":1,"Simulation::Aero::simulate_aero":1,"Simulation::Aero::post_refine_aero":1,"Simulation::Aero::source_fuel":1,"Simulation::Aero::aero_combustion_settings":1,"Rendering::Arnold::Arnold_standard_volume_mat":1,"Simulation::Influence::ground_plane_influence":1,"Simulation::Influence::modulate_influence":1,"Simulation::Influence::dissipation_influence":1,"source_geometry":1,"noise":1,"Simulation::Influence::aero_pyroclastic_influence":1,"Modeling::Primitive::create_mesh_plane":1,"Rendering::Materials::standard_surface_mat":1,"Simulation::Common::file_cache":1,"Geometry::Properties::erase_component_properties":1,"calculate_simulation_speed":1,"Simulation::Common::compute_velocity":1,"Modeling::Primitive::create_mesh_torus":1,"Modeling::Points::displace_points":1,"Core::Graph::terminal":1,"Core::Fields::fractal_turbulence_field":1,"Core::Fields::scalar_field":1,"Core::Fields::vector_field":1},"edge_type_pairs":{" -> Core::Fields::fractal_turbulence_field":4,"Simulation::Common::vary_source_property -> Simulation::Aero::