ude","type":"float","value":"1f"},{"path":"root/noise","name":"fractal_turbulence_field1.num_frequencies","type":"float","value":"6f"},{"path":"root/noise","name":"fractal_turbulence_field1.frequency","type":"float","value":"0.800000012f"},{"path":"root/noise","name":"fractal_turbulence_field1.ratio","type":"float","value":"0.450100005f"},{"path":"root/noise","name":"fractal_turbulence_field1.frequency_ratio","type":"float","value":"0.503283024f"},{"path":"root/noise","name":"fractal_turbulence_field1.seed","type":"long","value":"36"},{"path":"root/noise","name":"scalar_field.value","type":"float","value":"0f"},{"path":"root/noise","name":"vector_field.value","type":"Math::float3","value":{"x":"2f","y":"1.5f","z":"1f"}},{"path":"root/calculate_simulation_speed","name":"value.value","type":"float","value":"1f"},{"path":"root/calculate_simulation_speed","name":"value1.value","type":"float","value":"60f"}],"compounds":[{"path":"root","name":"Graphs::Aero::pyroclastic_explosion","metadata":{"compoundIsGraph":"true","author":"Autodesk","category":"Fire","description":"This graph shows how to use Bifrost Aero to create a large, pyroclastic explosion.","documentation":"pyroclastic_explosion_docs_${language}.md","_notes":[{"type":"sticky_note","text":"This compound generates an animated turbulence (3d) displacement field used for displacing the source object and controlling the direction of the initial speed for the explosion. \nDive inside for details."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"This compound takes the displacement field and uses it to displace a source geometry.  It also sets a velocity property on the object. \n"},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"This compound is very useful for varying properties on the incoming source object.  This one varies the `voxel_fog_density` property."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"The two Aero air sources for the explosion.  You can see that one of them is connected to a fuel source for fire and combustion, which the other is not - for smoke and dust effects."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"Aero fuel settings - controls all things fuel related for the combustion process."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"This is an aero influence, it influences the simulation.  This one is a pyroclastic influence, it adds pyroclastic details to the simulation that resemble explosions, volcanic eruptions etc.\nIt influences both the vorticity (small scale swirls and detail)  and the drag (how quickly the simulation slows down)"},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"The dissipation influence reduces a property over time, in this case it's the `voxel_fog_density` which gives the effect of the simulation \"fading\" into the background."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"Modulate influence allows you to use a simulation property to scale the amount of effect applied by other influence nodes.  This one uses `voxel_fog_density` to modulate the dissipation influence before it."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"These are the settings that are passed into the Aero solver.  They control all facets of the simulation.  Because this sim has a combustion element, `aero_combustion_settings` is connected through the `additional_settings` port.\naero_solver_settings is editable to set an increased voxel_padding. At the expense of solver speed and memory usage, the bounds are increased to accommodate the explosive expansion of the combusted fuel."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"This controls the resolution of the entire simulation, you can make this value bigger to get a faster, less accurate simulation for testing purposes."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"The file_cache compound will save the simulation data out to disk, it is set to passthrough.\nWhen running the simulation at production resolution it is ideal to cache out the simulation data before rendering, as typically the render will take longer than the simulation and can be easily resumed (unlike the simulation)"},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"When you are caching a simulation to disk, it is advisable to only cache the volume properties you will need to render the simulation, or in this case, post-refine the simulation.  This compound uses a space-separated expression to select which properties are deleted."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"Simulate Aero.  This compound takes in all the inputs and simulates the volumes."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"This compound adds high-resolution detail to your simulation after simulation.  This can give very good looking results to a lower-resolution simulation, generally faster than resimulating at higher settings."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"Assigning a standard_volume_material to the output volume ensures display in the viewport is as close to the final render as possible.\n\nNOTE:  The interpolation on the ***arnold_standard_volume_mat*** compound should be set to \"tricubic\" for best results."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"If you wish to render a ground plane with this example, plug this into the output."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"The frames per second (FPS) defined in a scene have a significant impact on simulation behavior. We can approximate the same behavior with varying frames per second, though, there will likely always be slight differences, say, between 60 and 24 fps, used often in games and film, respectively"}],"_backdrops":[{"type":"backdrop","title":"Fluid detail size"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Optional: ground plane"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Master Displacement Field"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Dummy Geo"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Vary fog density"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Vary temperature"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Aero Source"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Fuel"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Influences"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Time Adjustment"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Pyroclastic Influence"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Ground Plane"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Dissapation influence"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Combustion and Solver Settings"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"File Caching"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Simulate!"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Refine the Volumes"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Assign a material"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Simulation speed based on FPS"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Aero"}]},"ports":[{"portName":"aero_volume","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"}],"terminal_states":[{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::final","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::proxy","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::diagnostic","enabled":"false"}],"node_count":32,"connection_count":37,"value_count":120},{"path":"root/source_geometry","name":"source_geometry","metadata":{"_notes":[{"type":"sticky_note","text":"This is the geometry used to generate the explosion.  You can replace this with a mesh of your own if desired."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"This uses the field calculated outside this compound to displace the source geometry over time."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"This computes the velocities between two frames for a given geometry, and stores it on the geometry as a property called `point_velocity`.  After the start frame has been reached."}],"_backdrops":[{"type":"backdrop","title":"Source Geometry"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Displacement"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Velocity"}]},"ports":[{"portName":"out_geometry","portDirection":"output"},{"portName":"displacement_field","portDirection":"input"},{"portName":"start_frame","portDirection":"input","portType":"float"}],"terminal_states":[],"node_count":5,"connection_count":7,"value_count":10},{"path":"root/noise","name":"noise","metadata":{"_notes":[{"type":"sticky_note","text":"Master time node, connecting the maya timeline to the Bifrost graph."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"Multiplying the frames from the time node allows you to control the speed of the displacement."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"The results need to be converted into a scalar field for input into the turbulence field."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"The magnitude of the displacement is the frame output of the time node.  This fCurve  adjusts it. "},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"Converts your speed input vector to a vector field for multiplication with the turbulence field (scaling). "},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"Based on your inputs, this generates the 3d (vector) field."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"Multiplies the field by the incoming scale vector, converted to field."}],"_backdrops":[{"type":"backdrop","title":"Frames"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Speed"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"To Field"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Magnitude adjustment"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"To Field"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Turbulence Field"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Field Scale"}]},"ports":[{"portName":"output","portDirection":"output"},{"portName":"scale","portDirection":"input","portType":"Math::float3"},{"portName":"speed","portDirection":"input","portType":"float"},{"portName":"animation","portDirection":"input","portType":"Math::FCurve"},{"portName":"num_frequencies","portDirection":"input","portType":"float"},{"portName":"frequency","portDirection":"input","portType":"float"},{"portName":"ratio","portDirection":"input","portType":"float"},{"portName":"frequency_ratio","portDirection":"input","portType":"float"}],"terminal_states":[],"node_count":7,"connection_count":15,"value_count":9},{"path":"root/calculate_simulation_speed","name":"calculate_simulation_speed","metadata":{"_notes":[{"type":"sticky_note","text":"The simulation was developed in a scene at 60 fps. Many users work at 24 fps, but any framerate could be present in a user scene. We use this compound to scale the simulation speed compared to our baseline of 60 fps.\nThe results will differ to some extent, but they should be relatively similar using this technique."}]},"ports":[{"portName":"frame_length","portDirection":"input"},{"portName":"simulation_speed","portDirection":"output"}],"terminal_states":[],"node_count":4,"connection_cou